export const SM6={title:'Examination and assessment I: evaluation process and lower-extremity injuries',hook:'An athlete limps off the field holding her knee. In the next few minutes, the athletic trainer must decide whether she can return, needs a brace and ice or needs to go to the emergency room. A systematic evaluation—history, observation, palpation and special tests—makes that decision safe. Examination, evaluation and assessment is 30% of the test.',objectives:['Use the HOPS evaluation method and grade injuries.','Describe the body’s response to injury and the signs of inflammation.','Recognize ankle, lower-leg and knee injuries and their special tests.','Apply the Ottawa ankle rules and decide when to refer.'],sections:[
['The evaluation process',[
'Athletic trainers use **HOPS**: **H**istory (what happened, mechanism of injury, location and type of pain, sounds such as a “pop,” previous injuries), **O**bservation (swelling, deformity, discoloration, gait, willingness to bear weight, comparison with the other side), **P**alpation (point tenderness, deformity, temperature, crepitus—always palpating the uninjured side and away from the injury first) and **S**pecial tests (ROM, strength, ligament stress tests, neurological and circulatory checks). An on-field primary survey rules out life-threatening problems first.',
'Injuries are classified as **acute** (sudden trauma) or **chronic/overuse** (repetitive stress). **Sprains** (ligaments) and **strains** (muscles and tendons) are graded **I** (stretching, mild pain, no laxity), **II** (partial tear, moderate pain, some laxity) and **III** (complete tear, significant laxity, sometimes less pain because nerves tear too). **Contusions** are bruises; **fractures** may be closed, open, stress or avulsion. The evaluation ends with a decision: return to play, modify activity, refer to a physician or activate EMS.',
order('HOPS',['History: mechanism, pain, sounds, prior injury','Observation: swelling, deformity, gait, compare sides','Palpation: start away from the injury; point tenderness','Special tests: ROM, strength, stress tests, neurovascular','Decide: return, modify, refer or activate EMS'])]],
['Response to injury',[
'Tissue injury triggers **inflammation**, with the cardinal signs of **redness, heat, swelling, pain and loss of function**. The **acute inflammatory phase** (first few days) brings blood vessel changes, swelling and pain; the **fibroblastic repair (proliferative) phase** (days to weeks) lays down new collagen; and the **maturation-remodeling phase** (weeks to a year or more) strengthens and aligns tissue along lines of stress. Too much swelling slows healing, which is why early care uses protection, ice, compression and elevation.',
'Bone heals through hematoma formation, soft callus, hard callus and remodeling, typically over **6–12 weeks** in adults. Tendons and ligaments heal slowly because of limited blood supply. Pain is also affected by the athlete’s psychology—fear, anxiety and motivation—so sports psychologists and supportive communication help.',
match('Healing phase',[['Inflammatory phase','Redness, heat, swelling and pain in the first days'],['Fibroblastic repair','New collagen laid down'],['Maturation-remodeling','Collagen aligned along lines of stress'],['Callus','Bridges a fracture as bone heals'],['Grade I sprain','Stretch with no laxity'],['Grade III sprain','Complete tear with significant laxity']])]],
['Ankle and lower-leg injuries',[
'**Lateral (inversion) ankle sprains** are the most common sports injury, usually injuring the **ATFL** first and then the CFL. The **anterior drawer test** checks the ATFL; the **talar tilt (inversion stress) test** checks the CFL. **Medial (eversion) sprains** are less common but more severe. **High ankle sprains** (syndesmosis) result from external rotation and are checked with the squeeze or external rotation test. The **Ottawa ankle rules** recommend X-rays if there is **bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus** (distal 6 cm) or **inability to bear weight for four steps** both immediately and in the clinic; for the foot, tenderness at the **base of the fifth metatarsal** or the **navicular**.',
'**Achilles tendinopathy** causes gradual pain; **Achilles rupture** causes a sudden “kicked in the calf” feeling and a positive **Thompson test**. **Medial tibial stress syndrome** (shin splints) causes diffuse pain along the inner tibia from overuse; **stress fractures** cause focal bone pain; **exertional compartment syndrome** causes pain and tightness that resolve with rest, while **acute compartment syndrome** after trauma—pain out of proportion, especially with passive stretch—is an emergency.',
match('Ankle test and structure',[['Anterior drawer of the ankle','ATFL'],['Talar tilt (inversion stress)','Calcaneofibular ligament'],['Squeeze test','Syndesmosis (high ankle sprain)'],['Thompson test','Achilles tendon rupture'],['Ottawa ankle rules','When to X-ray an ankle injury'],['Pain with passive stretch after trauma','Acute compartment syndrome']])]],
['Knee injuries',[
'**ACL tears** usually happen without contact during **sudden deceleration, cutting, pivoting or landing**, often with a “pop,” rapid swelling and instability; the **Lachman test** (most sensitive) and **anterior drawer test** check the ACL. **MCL sprains** come from a **valgus (abduction) force with rotation on a planted foot**, checked with the **valgus stress test**; **LCL sprains** from varus force (varus stress test). **PCL injuries** follow **falling on a flexed knee** or dashboard impact (posterior drawer, sag sign). The **“unhappy triad”**—ACL, MCL and medial meniscus—can occur together.',
'**Meniscus tears** cause joint-line pain, catching or locking and swelling; the **McMurray test** and **Thessaly test** help detect them. **Patellar dislocation** (usually lateral) and **patellofemoral pain** relate to tracking. **Patellar tendinopathy** (“jumper’s knee”) and **Osgood-Schlatter disease** (tibial tuberosity apophysitis in adolescents) are overuse problems. Refer any knee with gross instability, locking, rapid large swelling or inability to bear weight.',
"**Neurovascular checks** are part of every lower-extremity evaluation: compare pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial), skin color and temperature, capillary refill and sensation on both sides. A cold, pale foot or loss of sensation after a knee dislocation is a limb-threatening emergency because the popliteal artery may be damaged.",
sort('Which knee structure does the test check?',['ACL','MCL','PCL','Meniscus'],[['Lachman test',0],['Valgus stress test',1],['Posterior drawer',2],['McMurray test',3],['Anterior drawer of the knee',0],['Sag sign',2]])]],
['Hip, thigh and overuse injuries',[
'**Quadriceps contusions** (“charley horse”) can cause **myositis ossificans**—bone forming in muscle—if treated with heat or massage too early; treat with ice and gentle stretch in knee flexion. **Hamstring strains** are common in sprinters and often recur, so eccentric strengthening (Nordic curls) is a key prevention. **Hip flexor strains**, **groin (adductor) strains** and **hip pointers** (iliac crest contusions) are also common.',
'**Overuse injuries** develop from repetitive stress with too little recovery: stress fractures, tendinopathies, iliotibial band syndrome and apophysitis in growing athletes. Training errors (doing “too much, too soon”), poor footwear, biomechanics and low energy availability contribute. Management includes relative rest, correcting causes and gradual return.',
match('Hip and thigh',[['Quadriceps contusion','Ice and stretch in knee flexion; avoid early heat'],['Myositis ossificans','Bone forming in muscle after a deep bruise'],['Hamstring strain','Common in sprinters; eccentric strengthening'],['Hip pointer','Bruise on the iliac crest'],['Iliotibial band syndrome','Lateral knee pain in runners'],['Stress fracture','Focal bone pain from repetitive loading']])]],
['Practice evaluation',[
kase('Sideline knee evaluation',{a:{text:'A soccer player planted her foot, cut and felt a pop. Her knee swelled within an hour. Which test is most sensitive for her likely injury? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Lachman test for the ACL','b','Correct. Noncontact cutting with a pop and rapid swelling suggests an ACL tear.',true],['Thompson test','b','That checks the Achilles.',false]]},b:{text:'The Lachman shows increased laxity with a soft end feel compared with the other knee. What next?',choices:[['Remove from play, apply ice, compression and crutches as needed, and refer to the physician','c','Correct. Suspected ACL tear needs physician evaluation and imaging.',true],['Tape the knee and return her to play','c','An unstable knee must not return to play.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the mechanism, findings and referral and inform her parents.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['HOPS (history, observation, palpation, special tests); grade sprains and strains I–III; decide return, modify, refer or activate EMS.','Ankle: inversion sprains injure the ATFL (anterior drawer) and CFL (talar tilt); Ottawa rules guide X-rays; Thompson test for Achilles rupture; watch for compartment syndrome.','Knee: ACL (deceleration/cutting; Lachman), MCL (valgus with rotation on a planted foot; valgus stress), PCL (fall on flexed knee; posterior drawer), meniscus (McMurray); quad contusions risk myositis ossificans.'],terms:[['HOPS','History, observation, palpation and special tests.'],['Lachman test','The most sensitive clinical test for an ACL tear.'],['Ottawa ankle rules','Guidelines for when an ankle injury needs an X-ray.']],mcqs:[q('What is a common mechanism of injury for an MCL sprain?','Abduction and lower leg rotation on a planted foot','Quick deceleration, cutting, twisting and landing','Falling on the anterior knee while flexed','Adduction and internal rotation of the knee','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which test is most sensitive for an ACL tear?','Lachman test','McMurray test','Thompson test','Valgus stress test','Compare with the other knee.'),q('Falling on a flexed knee most often injures the:','PCL','ACL','MCL','ATFL','Posterior cruciate.'),q('The talar tilt test checks the:','Calcaneofibular ligament','ATFL only','Deltoid ligament','Achilles tendon','Inversion stress.'),q('In HOPS, the S stands for:','Special tests','Swelling','Splinting','Strength only','History, observation, palpation, special tests.'),q('Which finding under the Ottawa rules calls for an ankle X-ray?','Inability to bear weight for four steps','Mild swelling only','Bruising on the shin','Pain only with running','Or malleolar bone tenderness.'),q('Early heat or massage on a deep quadriceps bruise risks:','Myositis ossificans','Osgood-Schlatter disease','Plantar fasciitis','Shin splints','Bone in muscle.'),q('A grade III sprain is:','A complete ligament tear','A mild stretch','Not an injury','A muscle strain','Significant laxity.')],frq:{prompt:'A basketball player lands on another player’s foot and rolls her ankle inward. Describe how you would evaluate her using HOPS, which special tests you would use and how you would decide whether she needs an X-ray or can return to play.',rubric:[['History and observation',['mechanism','inversion','pop','previous','swelling','deformity','bear weight','compare']],['Palpation',['uninjured side','away','atfl','calcaneofibular','malleolus','fifth metatarsal','navicular']],['Special tests',['anterior drawer','talar tilt','squeeze','range of motion','strength','neurovascular']],['Decision',['ottawa','x-ray','four steps','grade','refer','return','functional']]],model:'I start with the history: she rolled her ankle into inversion when she landed on another foot. I ask whether she heard a pop, where the pain is, whether she could keep playing and whether she has sprained it before. On observation, I look for swelling and bruising around the lateral malleolus, deformity and whether she can bear weight, always comparing with the other ankle. I palpate the uninjured side first and then begin away from the most painful area, feeling the ATFL in front of the lateral malleolus, the calcaneofibular ligament below it, the posterior edges and tips of both malleoli, the base of the fifth metatarsal and the navicular. Special tests include active, passive and resisted range of motion, an anterior drawer test for the ATFL, a talar tilt test for the calcaneofibular ligament, a squeeze test to rule out a high ankle sprain and checks of pulses and sensation. Using the Ottawa ankle rules, I refer her for an X-ray if she has bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of either malleolus, at the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular, or if she cannot bear weight for four steps. If no fracture is suspected, I grade the sprain; she returns to play only when she has full range of motion, normal strength and balance and can complete sport-specific functional tests without pain, often with tape or a brace.'},sources:[RULES,NIAMS_SPRAIN,AAOS_ACL,NATA]};

export const SM7={title:'Examination and assessment II: upper extremity, head and spine',hook:'A quarterback falls on his shoulder and the end of his collarbone sticks up. A gymnast falls on an outstretched hand. A soccer player seems dazed after a header and asks the same question three times. Upper-extremity, head and spine injuries range from minor to life-threatening, and recognizing the difference is essential.',objectives:['Recognize shoulder injuries, including AC sprains, dislocations and rotator cuff problems.','Recognize elbow, wrist and hand injuries.','Identify concussion signs and symptoms and apply return-to-learn and return-to-play steps.','Recognize cervical spine injuries and other head and face injuries.'],sections:[
['Shoulder injuries',[
'An **acromioclavicular (AC) sprain** (“shoulder separation”) usually results from falling directly on the point of the shoulder; higher grades show a **step deformity** where the clavicle rides up. **Clavicle fractures** follow falls on the shoulder or outstretched arm; the athlete supports the arm and has deformity along the collarbone. **Glenohumeral dislocations** are most often **anterior**, caused by abduction and external rotation (as in arm tackling); the shoulder looks flattened, the arm is held slightly away from the body and the athlete is in severe pain. Do not attempt reduction unless trained and authorized—splint in a comfortable position, check circulation and nerves (axillary nerve sensation over the deltoid) and refer.',
'**Rotator cuff** strains and tears and **impingement** cause pain with overhead activity, often in swimmers, throwers and tennis players. Special tests include the **empty can (Jobe) test** for the supraspinatus, **Neer** and **Hawkins-Kennedy** tests for impingement and the **apprehension test** for anterior instability. **SLAP (labral) tears** cause deep shoulder pain and clicking in throwers.',
match('Shoulder test and problem',[['Empty can test','Supraspinatus strength'],['Neer and Hawkins-Kennedy tests','Impingement'],['Apprehension test','Anterior shoulder instability'],['Step deformity at the outer clavicle','AC sprain'],['Flattened shoulder after abduction and external rotation','Anterior dislocation'],['Numb patch over the deltoid','Possible axillary nerve injury']])]],
['Elbow, wrist and hand injuries',[
'**Medial elbow pain** in throwers suggests a **UCL sprain** (valgus stress test, moving valgus test) or **medial epicondyle apophysitis** (“Little League elbow”) in young pitchers—pitch counts and rest prevent it. **Lateral epicondylitis** (tennis elbow) causes pain with gripping and resisted wrist extension. **Elbow dislocations** follow falls on an outstretched hand and require immediate neurovascular checks and referral. **Olecranon bursitis** causes swelling at the point of the elbow.',
'A **fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH)** can fracture the **distal radius** or the **scaphoid**—**snuffbox tenderness** means splinting and referral even if the first X-ray is normal. A **boxer’s fracture** affects the fifth metacarpal neck. **Mallet finger** (the fingertip droops after a ball strikes it) needs continuous splinting in extension; **jersey finger** (inability to bend the fingertip after grabbing a jersey) needs surgical referral; **gamekeeper’s (skier’s) thumb** is a sprain of the thumb’s UCL. Dislocated fingers should not be “popped back” by untrained people—splint and refer.',
match('Upper-extremity injury',[['Snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH','Possible scaphoid fracture'],['Fingertip droops after a ball strike','Mallet finger'],['Cannot bend the fingertip after grabbing a jersey','Jersey finger'],['Thumb pulled back on a ski pole','Gamekeeper’s/skier’s thumb'],['Pain with gripping and resisted wrist extension','Lateral epicondylitis'],['Medial elbow pain in a young pitcher','Little League elbow']])]],
['Concussion',[
'A **concussion** is a mild traumatic brain injury caused by a blow or jolt that moves the brain inside the skull. Most occur **without loss of consciousness**. Signs and symptoms include **headache**, dizziness, nausea, confusion, memory problems, sensitivity to light and noise, blurred vision, balance problems, feeling “foggy,” irritability and sleep changes. Assessment tools include symptom checklists, cognitive screens, balance tests and the **SCAT6** (Sport Concussion Assessment Tool), compared with baseline when available.',
'Any athlete with a suspected concussion is **removed from play and does not return the same day**. **Red flags** that require emergency care: worsening headache, repeated vomiting, seizures, increasing confusion, unequal pupils, weakness or numbness, slurred speech, neck pain or loss of consciousness. Recovery follows **return to learn** (gradual return to school with accommodations) and a stepwise **return to play**: symptom-limited activity, light aerobic exercise, sport-specific exercise, non-contact drills, full-contact practice after medical clearance and then competition, typically with **at least 24 hours between steps**. **Second impact syndrome**—a second hit before recovery—can cause catastrophic brain swelling in young athletes.',
order('Graduated return to play',['Symptom-limited daily activity','Light aerobic exercise','Sport-specific exercise','Non-contact training drills','Full-contact practice after medical clearance','Return to competition']),
sort('Concussion: monitor or emergency?',['Monitor and follow concussion protocol','Emergency—call EMS'],[['Mild headache and feeling foggy',0],['Repeated vomiting',1],['Seizure',1],['Sensitivity to light',0],['Unequal pupils',1],['Increasing confusion and drowsiness',1]])]],
['Spine, face and other head injuries',[
'Suspect a **cervical spine injury** with any head-first contact (spearing), neck pain, numbness or tingling in the arms or legs, weakness or an unconscious athlete. Keep the head and neck **still in line** (manual stabilization), check breathing and circulation, activate EMS and follow the EAP for **spine motion restriction**; trained personnel remove a football **face mask** to access the airway, and equipment removal follows current guidelines with enough trained rescuers. A **“burner” or “stinger”** is a stretch or compression of the brachial plexus causing burning pain down one arm that usually resolves in minutes; symptoms in both arms, or that persist, suggest spinal cord involvement.',
'**Epidural hematomas** may cause a “lucid interval” before rapid decline; **subdural hematomas** may develop more slowly—both are emergencies. Facial injuries include **nosebleeds** (lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose), **nasal fractures**, **orbital fractures** (double vision, limited eye movement), **dental injuries** (a knocked-out permanent tooth is handled by the crown, rinsed briefly and replanted or stored in milk or a tooth-preservation solution, with dental care within 30–60 minutes) and **eye injuries** requiring immediate referral.',
match('Head, face and spine',[['Burning pain down one arm after a hit','Burner/stinger'],['Tingling in both arms after spearing','Possible cervical spinal cord injury'],['Lucid interval then rapid decline','Epidural hematoma'],['Nosebleed first aid','Lean forward and pinch the soft nose'],['Knocked-out tooth','Handle by the crown; store in milk'],['Double vision after a blow to the eye','Possible orbital fracture']])]],
['Practice evaluation',[
kase('Header collision',{a:{text:'A soccer player collides heads with an opponent. She did not lose consciousness but is dizzy, has a headache and asks what the score is three times. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Remove her from play for the rest of the day and perform a concussion assessment','b','Correct. Suspected concussion means no same-day return.',true],['Let her return if she feels better in 15 minutes','b','No same-day return to play.',false]]},b:{text:'An hour later she vomits twice and her headache is worsening. What now?',choices:[['Call EMS—these are red flags','c','Correct. Worsening symptoms need emergency evaluation.',true],['Give her ibuprofen and send her home alone','c','Red flags require emergency care, and she should not be alone.',false]]},c:{text:'She is transported, and later begins return-to-learn and a stepwise return-to-play protocol after medical clearance.',end:true}})]],
["Other upper-body and torso conditions",["**Rib contusions and fractures** cause sharp pain with breathing; difficulty breathing or decreased breath sounds may mean a **pneumothorax**, which needs emergency care. **Spleen** injuries (left upper abdomen, with pain referred to the left shoulder) are a risk after hard hits, and athletes with **mononucleosis** should avoid contact sports—usually for at least 3–4 weeks—because the spleen may be enlarged.","**Overuse injuries** in throwers and swimmers develop from repetitive overhead motion: **pitch counts**, rest days, proper mechanics, rotator cuff and scapular strengthening and avoiding year-round single-sport specialization reduce risk. **Thoracic outlet syndrome** and **nerve entrapments** cause tingling in the arm and need referral.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Refer urgently or manage and monitor?","buckets":["Refer urgently","Manage and monitor"],"items":[["Shortness of breath after a rib injury",0],["Left shoulder pain after a hit to the abdomen",0],["Mild shoulder soreness after pitching practice",1],["Contact sport 1 week after mononucleosis diagnosis",0],["Small bruise on the forearm",1],["Mild tennis elbow pain with gripping",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Shoulder: AC sprain (step deformity), clavicle fracture, anterior dislocation (abduction and external rotation; check the axillary nerve), rotator cuff and impingement (empty can, Neer, Hawkins-Kennedy).','Elbow and hand: UCL and Little League elbow in throwers, tennis elbow, FOOSH injuries (snuffbox tenderness = scaphoid), boxer’s fracture, mallet and jersey finger, gamekeeper’s thumb.','Concussion: remove from play, no same-day return, watch red flags, return to learn and stepwise return to play with medical clearance; protect the cervical spine and manage facial and dental injuries.'],terms:[['Concussion','A mild traumatic brain injury from a blow or jolt.'],['Second impact syndrome','Dangerous brain swelling from a second blow before recovery.'],['FOOSH','Fall on an outstretched hand.']],mcqs:[q('Most anterior shoulder dislocations occur with the arm in:','Abduction and external rotation','Adduction and internal rotation','Full flexion','Neutral at the side','Arm tackling.'),q('Snuffbox tenderness after a fall suggests:','Scaphoid fracture','Boxer’s fracture','Mallet finger','Tennis elbow','Splint and refer.'),q('Which test checks the supraspinatus?','Empty can (Jobe) test','Lachman test','Thompson test','McMurray test','Rotator cuff.'),q('After a suspected concussion, the athlete:','Does not return to play the same day','May return after 15 minutes','Returns if they pass one question','Must keep playing','No same-day return.'),q('Which is a concussion red flag?','Repeated vomiting','Mild headache','Feeling tired','Sensitivity to light','Call EMS.'),q('A knocked-out permanent tooth should be handled by the:','Crown','Root','Gums','Nerve','Store in milk if not replanted.'),q('Burning pain down one arm that resolves in minutes is a:','Burner/stinger','Spinal cord injury always','Heart attack','Dislocated finger','Brachial plexus.'),q('Mallet finger is treated by:','Continuous splinting in extension','Immediate surgery always','Ice only','Taping two fingers together','Weeks in a splint.')],frq:{prompt:'A football player makes a head-first tackle and stays down. He is conscious but reports neck pain and tingling in both hands. Explain your immediate actions and how they differ from caring for a player with a burner or a concussion.',rubric:[['Spine precautions',['manual stabilization','in line','do not move','eap','ems','spine motion restriction']],['Assessment',['airway','breathing','circulation','sensation','strength','both arms','face mask']],['Burner comparison',['burner','one arm','brachial plexus','resolves','minutes']],['Concussion comparison',['concussion','remove','no same-day','scat','red flags','return to play']]],model:'Neck pain with tingling in both hands after a head-first tackle suggests a cervical spine injury, so I treat it as an emergency. I tell him not to move, kneel at his head and manually stabilize the head and neck in line, activate the emergency action plan and call EMS. I monitor his airway, breathing and circulation; because he is conscious and breathing, I leave the helmet and shoulder pads in place unless the EAP and trained rescuers call for removal, and I make sure the tools to remove the face mask for airway access are ready. I check sensation and strength in his arms and legs, reassure him and help with spine motion restriction and transport by trained personnel. A burner, by contrast, is a stretch or compression of the brachial plexus that causes burning pain and tingling down one arm and usually resolves within minutes; symptoms in both arms, neck pain or persistent weakness rule out a simple burner. A concussion involves symptoms such as headache, dizziness and confusion without spinal findings; that athlete is removed from play, assessed with a tool such as the SCAT6, monitored for red flags and not returned the same day, then follows a stepwise return to learn and return to play after medical clearance.'},sources:[RULES,AAOS_SHOULDER,AAOS_SCAPHOID,CDC_HEADS]};

export const SM8={title:'Immediate and emergency care',hook:'Sudden cardiac arrest is the leading cause of death in young athletes during exercise, and exertional heat stroke is one of the most preventable. In both, survival depends on what happens in the first minutes—an AED within three to five minutes, or a cold-water tub before transport. Immediate and emergency care is 16% of the test.',objectives:['Perform a primary survey and activate the emergency action plan.','Recognize and manage sudden cardiac arrest, including CPR and AED use.','Recognize and treat heat illnesses, exertional sickling and other medical emergencies.','Control bleeding, splint fractures and recognize shock.'],sections:[
['The primary and secondary survey',[
'On arrival, ensure **scene safety** and use standard precautions. The **primary survey** finds life-threatening problems: **responsiveness**, **airway**, **breathing** and **circulation** (including severe bleeding). If the athlete is unresponsive and not breathing normally, **activate EMS and get the AED** while starting CPR. Suspect a spinal injury with any head or neck trauma. The **secondary survey** follows once life threats are managed: a focused history (SAMPLE—Signs and symptoms, Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last oral intake, Events), vital signs and a head-to-toe exam.',
'The **emergency action plan** assigns roles: one person provides care, another calls 911 with the exact location, someone retrieves the AED and equipment, and someone meets EMS at the gate. Rehearsing the plan saves minutes.',
order('Primary survey',['Scene safety and gloves','Check responsiveness','Activate EMS and send for the AED if unresponsive','Open and check the airway','Check breathing and pulse (no more than 10 seconds)','Control severe bleeding; begin CPR if needed'])]],
['Sudden cardiac arrest, CPR and AED',[
'**Sudden cardiac arrest (SCA)** in young athletes is often caused by inherited heart conditions such as **hypertrophic cardiomyopathy** or electrical disorders, or by **commotio cordis**—a blow to the chest at a vulnerable moment of the heartbeat (for example, from a baseball or hockey puck). An athlete who collapses without contact and is unresponsive should be presumed to be in SCA. Brief, jerky movements or gasping (**agonal breathing**) may occur—do not mistake them for a seizure or normal breathing.',
'Start **high-quality CPR**: compressions at least **2 inches deep** (adults) at **100–120 per minute**, allowing full recoil, minimizing interruptions and giving **30 compressions to 2 breaths** for a single rescuer. Apply the **AED as soon as it arrives**—the goal is a shock within **3–5 minutes** of collapse—and follow its prompts. Survival falls roughly 7–10% for each minute without defibrillation. Schools with AEDs and trained staff save lives.',
flip('CPR and AED key numbers',[['Compression depth','At least 2 inches in adults'],['Compression rate','100–120 per minute'],['Ratio (single rescuer)','30 compressions : 2 breaths'],['AED goal','Shock within 3–5 minutes of collapse'],['Pulse check','No more than 10 seconds'],['Commotio cordis','Chest blow causing cardiac arrest']])]],
['Heat illness and exertional sickling',[
'**Heat cramps** are painful muscle spasms—treat with rest, stretching, fluids and sodium. **Heat exhaustion** causes fatigue, heavy sweating, nausea, headache and dizziness with a core temperature usually below 40 °C (104 °F) and normal mental status—move to shade, remove equipment, cool and hydrate, and do not return that day. **Exertional heat stroke** is a medical emergency: **core temperature above about 40 °C (104 °F)** with **central nervous system dysfunction** (confusion, irritability, collapse, seizures). Measure **rectal temperature**, the only accurate method during exercise.',
'Treat heat stroke with **immediate cold-water immersion**—“**cool first, transport second**”—aiming to bring the temperature down to about 38.9 °C (102 °F) within **30 minutes** of collapse, then transport. Survival approaches 100% with rapid cooling. **Exertional sickling** in athletes with **sickle cell trait** causes weakness and collapse during intense exercise, often early in conditioning, with muscles that feel weak rather than cramped and the athlete able to talk; it is an emergency—stop activity, give oxygen if available, monitor and activate EMS.',
sort('Which heat condition?',['Heat cramps','Heat exhaustion','Exertional heat stroke'],[['Painful calf spasms with normal mental status',0],['Heavy sweating, dizziness, temp 39 °C, alert',1],['Confusion and collapse, rectal temp 41 °C',2],['Needs cold-water immersion before transport',2],['Treat with shade, cooling and fluids; no return today',1],['Treat with stretching, fluids and sodium',0]])]],
['Other medical emergencies',[
'**Asthma attacks**: help the athlete use a rescue inhaler; call EMS for severe distress. **Anaphylaxis**: give **epinephrine** by auto-injector into the outer thigh and call EMS. **Hypoglycemia** in a diabetic athlete (shaky, sweaty, confused): give fast-acting sugar if they can swallow; unconscious athletes need EMS. **Seizures**: protect from injury, do not restrain or put anything in the mouth, time the seizure and call EMS if it lasts more than 5 minutes or another follows. **Lightning strikes**: victims do not carry a charge—begin CPR immediately.',
'**Shock** (pale, cool, clammy skin, rapid weak pulse, low blood pressure, anxiety) can follow bleeding, fractures, allergic reactions or heat. Lay the athlete flat, keep them warm, control bleeding and call EMS. **Internal injuries** to the spleen (left upper abdomen, pain referred to the left shoulder—Kehr’s sign) or kidneys (blood in urine) can follow hard hits.',
match('Emergency and first action',[['Anaphylaxis','Epinephrine auto-injector and EMS'],['Asthma attack','Rescue inhaler; EMS if severe'],['Conscious hypoglycemia','Fast-acting sugar'],['Seizure over 5 minutes','Call EMS'],['Lightning strike victim','Begin CPR immediately'],['Left shoulder pain after an abdominal hit','Possible spleen injury—refer urgently']])]],
['Bleeding, wounds and splinting',[
'Control bleeding with **direct pressure**; for life-threatening limb bleeding, apply a **tourniquet** 2–3 inches above the wound (not over a joint) and note the time, or pack deep wounds with gauze (**Stop the Bleed**). Clean minor wounds, cover them and watch for infection. **Blisters** and **abrasions** are cleaned and protected.',
'**Splint** suspected fractures and dislocations **in the position found** unless circulation is compromised, immobilizing the **joints above and below** a fracture (or the bones above and below a dislocated joint). Check **pulse, movement and sensation** before and after splinting. Vacuum, rigid and air splints are common in athletics. **Open fractures** are covered with a sterile dressing, and the bone is not pushed back in.',
order('Splinting steps',['Explain and expose the injury','Check circulation, movement and sensation below the injury','Splint in the position found','Immobilize the joints above and below a fracture','Recheck circulation, movement and sensation','Apply ice and elevate if appropriate; refer']),
kase('Collapse on the track',{a:{text:'A runner collapses without contact during an 800-meter race. He is unresponsive and gasping. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you assume?',choices:[['Sudden cardiac arrest—gasping is agonal breathing','b','Correct. Treat non-contact collapse as SCA.',true],['He fainted and will wake up—wait','b','Delay is deadly; start CPR.',false]]},b:{text:'What are the next actions?',choices:[['Call EMS, start CPR and apply the AED as soon as it arrives','c','Correct. Early CPR and defibrillation save lives.',true],['Check his blood sugar first','c','CPR and the AED come first.',false]]},c:{text:'The AED advises a shock. After one shock and CPR, EMS arrives and takes over care.',end:true}})]],
["Emergency preparedness in practice",["Emergency care works only if preparation happens first. Check that the **AED** battery and pads are current, the **cold-water tub** is filled before hot practices, a **rectal thermometer**, splints, spine-care equipment, face-mask removal tools, airway adjuncts and bleeding-control kits are on hand, and **communication** (phones, radios) works at every venue. Post emergency numbers and the venue address.","After any emergency, document what happened, review the response with the team and update the EAP. Support the athlete, teammates and staff, who may be upset after a serious event, and follow up with the athlete’s family and physician.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Ready or not ready?","buckets":["Ready","Not ready"],"items":[["AED pads within expiration date",0],["Cold-water tub filled before a hot practice",0],["Coach unsure of the field address",1],["Face-mask removal tool in the kit",0],["Phone with no signal and no backup radio",1],["EAP last rehearsed five years ago",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Scene safety, primary survey (responsiveness, airway, breathing, circulation), activate EMS and the AED; secondary survey with SAMPLE history and vital signs; rehearse the EAP.','SCA: presume it with non-contact collapse; CPR at least 2 inches deep at 100–120/min, 30:2, AED shock within 3–5 minutes; commotio cordis from chest blows.','Heat stroke (core >40 °C with CNS dysfunction) needs cold-water immersion before transport; treat exertional sickling, anaphylaxis, asthma, hypoglycemia and seizures as emergencies; control bleeding and splint joints above and below.'],terms:[['Commotio cordis','Cardiac arrest from a blow to the chest at a vulnerable moment.'],['Exertional heat stroke','Core temperature above about 40 °C with central nervous system dysfunction during exercise.'],['Agonal breathing','Gasping that occurs during cardiac arrest and is not normal breathing.']],mcqs:[q('What is the best treatment for exertional heat stroke?','Immediate cold-water immersion before transport','Transport first, cool later','Warm fluids','Rest in the sun','Cool first, transport second.'),q('Which temperature method is accurate during exercise?','Rectal','Oral','Tympanic','Forehead strip','Gold standard.'),q('The goal for AED shock after sudden cardiac arrest is within:','3–5 minutes','30 minutes','1 hour','10 seconds','Every minute counts.'),q('Adult CPR compression depth is at least:','2 inches','1 inch','4 inches','Half an inch','Rate 100–120/min.'),q('A blow to the chest causing cardiac arrest is:','Commotio cordis','Commotio retinae','Concussion','Pneumothorax only','Use the AED.'),q('How should a suspected fracture be splinted?','In the position found, immobilizing the joints above and below','Straightened forcefully first','Not at all','Only the injured bone','Check circulation.'),q('Which is the first treatment for anaphylaxis?','Epinephrine auto-injector','Antihistamine only','Ice','Inhaler','Then call EMS.'),q('Gasping in an unresponsive athlete who collapsed should be treated as:','Cardiac arrest','Normal breathing','A seizure','Exhaustion','Agonal breathing.')],frq:{prompt:'During a hot preseason practice, a lineman becomes confused and collapses. His rectal temperature is 41.4 °C (106.5 °F). Explain your immediate care, how it differs from heat exhaustion, and how the EAP supports the response.',rubric:[['Recognition',['exertional heat stroke','40','104','confusion','central nervous system','rectal']],['Cooling',['cold-water immersion','cool first','transport second','30 minutes','38.9','102','monitor']],['Heat exhaustion contrast',['heat exhaustion','below 40','alert','shade','fluids','no return']],['EAP and follow-up',['call ems','roles','tub','equipment','airway','physician','return gradually','prevention']]],model:'A core temperature of 41.4 °C with confusion and collapse during exercise in the heat is exertional heat stroke, a medical emergency. I activate the emergency action plan: one person calls EMS with the exact location, others bring the cold-water immersion tub and help, and someone meets the ambulance. I remove his equipment and immediately immerse his body and limbs in cold water, keeping his head and airway supported out of the water, stirring the water and adding ice. Following “cool first, transport second,” I continue cooling while monitoring rectal temperature, airway, breathing and pulse, aiming to reach about 38.9 °C (102 °F) within 30 minutes of collapse, then remove him from the tub and allow transport, because rapid cooling makes survival likely. Heat exhaustion differs: the athlete is fatigued, sweating and dizzy, but the core temperature is usually below 40 °C and mental status stays normal, so treatment is moving to shade, removing equipment, cooling, giving fluids and no return that day. The rehearsed EAP ensures the tub, thermometer and staff are ready, assigns roles and gets EMS to the right gate quickly. Afterward, the athlete needs physician clearance and a gradual return with heat acclimatization, and the team reviews prevention measures.'},sources:[RULES,NATA_HEAT,KSI,AHA_CPR,STOP_BLEED,NATA_SICKLE]};

export const SM9={title:'Therapeutic intervention and taping the ankle and Achilles (Skills III-A and III-B)',hook:'An athlete with a sprained ankle wants to be back for next week’s game. A rehabilitation plan—protecting healing tissue, restoring motion and strength, rebuilding balance and sport skills—and the right tape job make that possible without reinjury. Therapeutic intervention is 16% of the test, and taping is the heart of Round Two.',objectives:['Describe the phases of rehabilitation and return-to-play criteria.','Explain the uses and precautions of common therapeutic modalities.','Apply general taping principles, including preparation and removal.','Tape an ankle for inversion and the Achilles tendon (technique two) following HOSA Skills III-A and III-B.'],sections:[
['Rehabilitation phases and modalities',[
'Rehabilitation progresses through phases: **(1) acute/protection**—control pain and swelling with **PRICE or POLICE** (protection, optimal loading, ice, compression, elevation), protect healing tissue and keep the rest of the body fit; **(2) restoring motion**—active and passive ROM and flexibility; **(3) strength and endurance**—isometric, then isotonic (concentric and eccentric), then functional strengthening; **(4) neuromuscular control and proprioception**—balance boards, single-leg work, agility; **(5) sport-specific and functional training**—running, cutting, jumping and drills. **Return to play** requires full ROM, near-normal strength (often at least 90% of the other side), passing functional tests, no pain or swelling, psychological readiness and physician clearance.',
'**Modalities** support rehab: **cryotherapy** (ice bags, ice massage, cold whirlpool) reduces pain, swelling and spasm—typically 15–20 minutes; **thermotherapy** (moist heat, warm whirlpool) increases blood flow and tissue extensibility for chronic stiffness but not acute injuries; **therapeutic ultrasound** heats deep tissue; **electrical stimulation** (TENS for pain, NMES for muscle activation); **compression** devices reduce swelling; and **massage** and soft-tissue work aid recovery. Precautions include impaired sensation or circulation, cold sensitivity, open wounds, infections and pacemakers (for e-stim).',
order('Phases of rehabilitation',['Protection: control pain and swelling','Restore range of motion','Restore strength and endurance','Restore balance and neuromuscular control','Sport-specific functional training','Return to play after clearance'])]],
['Taping principles',[
'Tape **supports** joints, **limits harmful motion**, holds pads or dressings and improves **proprioception**. Before taping: obtain instructions, clean and dry the skin, check for allergies and skin problems, position the joint correctly, use **tape adherent** to help the tape stick (in HOSA skills you **demonstrate spraying without actually spraying** the patient), apply **thin foam pads with skin lubricant** over friction areas (heel and lace pads for the ankle) and apply **one thin, smooth layer of pre-wrap** (underwrap).',
'**Non-elastic (white) tape** restricts motion; **elastic tape** supports while allowing more movement and conforms to muscle. Apply tape with **mild to moderate, even roll tension**, let it **follow the natural contour** of the skin, overlap strips by about **one-half the tape width**, avoid wrinkles and gaps, and never encircle a muscle so tightly that circulation is cut off. Check circulation after taping (color, warmth, capillary refill, sensation). **Remove tape** with **bandage scissors** or a tape cutter (“shark”), cutting along the side away from bony prominences, then peel back against the skin, and check the skin for irritation.',
flip('Taping rules',[['Tape adherent','Demonstrate spraying in the event, do not actually spray'],['Heel and lace pads','Thin foam with skin lubricant to prevent blisters'],['Pre-wrap','One thin, smooth layer'],['Tension','Mild to moderate, even roll tension'],['Overlap','About one-half the tape width'],['Removal','Bandage scissors or tape cutter, then peel back']])]],
['Ankle taping for inversion: Skill III-A setup',[
'Skill III-A has 31 steps in **5 minutes**. **1–5.** Obtain instructions from the head athletic trainer (judge), assemble equipment, hand hygiene, greet, introduce yourself, identify the patient and explain. **6.** Position the patient **sitting on the taping table** with the leg extended off the edge and the **foot in dorsiflexion** (about 90°). **7.** Demonstrate spraying tape adherent. **8.** Apply **thin foam pads with skin lubricant** over the **heel and lace** areas. **9.** Apply **one thin, smooth layer of pre-wrap** to the foot and ankle.',
'**10.** With **non-elastic tape**, apply **two anchor strips** at a slight angle around the distal lower leg, **just below the belly of the gastrocnemius**. **11.** Apply an anchor around the **midfoot**, **proximal to the fifth metatarsal head**. These anchors give the stirrups and horseshoes a place to attach without sticking to skin.',
order('Ankle taping setup',['Sit with the leg off the table, foot in dorsiflexion','Demonstrate spraying tape adherent','Heel and lace pads with skin lubricant','One smooth layer of pre-wrap','Two angled anchors below the gastrocnemius belly','Midfoot anchor proximal to the fifth metatarsal head'])]],
['Stirrups, horseshoes, closures and heel locks: steps 12–31',[
'**12.** First **stirrup**: start on the **medial** lower-leg anchor, go down over the posterior medial malleolus, across the **plantar** foot and up over the posterior lateral malleolus to the **lateral** anchor—pulling the foot into **eversion** to resist inversion. **13.** First **horseshoe**: start on the medial midfoot anchor, go around the distal Achilles, across the distal lateral malleolus and finish on the lateral midfoot anchor. **14–17.** Alternate a **second and third stirrup** and **second and third horseshoe**, each overlapping the previous by **½ the tape width** and moving anteriorly (the closed basket weave). **18.** Apply **closure strips** from the third horseshoe upward, overlapping by ½, to the lower-leg anchor. **19.** Apply **2–3 closure strips around the midfoot**, medial to lateral. **20.** Make sure no tape presses on the **fifth metatarsal head**.',
'**21.** First **heel lock**: anchor across the lateral lace area, angle toward the medial longitudinal arch, cross the arch, angle upward across the **lateral calcaneus**, around the **posterior heel** and finish on the lateral lace area. **22.** Repeat on the other side in the opposite direction. **23.** Apply **2–3 heel locks** (individual or continuous). **24.** Report to the head athletic trainer, who evaluates the tape job. **25.** On direction, remove the tape with bandage scissors or a tape cutter. **26–31.** Mild to moderate roll tension, tape fits the natural contour, correct side and materials, proper disposal, hand hygiene and communication.',
order('Closed basket weave sequence',['Stirrup 1 (medial to lateral)','Horseshoe 1','Stirrup 2, overlapping ½','Horseshoe 2, overlapping ½','Stirrup 3','Horseshoe 3','Closure strips up the leg','Midfoot closure strips','Heel locks on both sides'])]],
['Achilles tendon taping: Skill III-B (technique two)',[
'Skill III-B has 25 steps in **6 minutes**. After the usual introductory steps: **6.** Position the patient **prone or kneeling** on the taping table with the lower leg off the edge. **7.** Place a hand on the plantar surface of the distal foot and **slowly dorsiflex** until the patient reports pain. **8.** Place the foot in the **pain-free range** (slight plantarflexion) and **maintain** that position during taping. **9.** Demonstrate spraying adherent on the lower leg and plantar foot. **10.** Place a thin **foam pad over the heel**. **11.** Apply pre-wrap. **12.** Apply **two anchors around the upper lower leg**, just below the knee around the upper gastrocnemius belly. **13.** Place an anchor around the **ball of the foot**.',
'**14.** Anchor a strip of **heavyweight elastic tape** on the mid-to-distal **plantar foot**, run it over the **middle calcaneus** and finish on the lower-leg anchor. **15.** Anchor the next strip at an angle over the **head of the fifth metatarsal**, cross over the **medial calcaneus** and finish on the **medial** lower-leg anchor. **16.** Place the last strip at an angle over the **head of the first metatarsal**, cross the **lateral calcaneus** and finish on the **lateral** anchor (forming an X behind the heel). **17.** Apply **2–3 circular strips** of elastic tape around the foot. **18.** Apply **4–6 strips** of elastic tape around the lower leg. **19–25.** Report to the head athletic trainer, remove with scissors on direction, ensure the tape allows normal, **pain-free** Achilles motion with support, correct side and materials, disposal, hand hygiene and communication.',
order('Achilles technique two',['Find the painful dorsiflexion point; hold the foot in the pain-free range','Heel pad and pre-wrap','Anchors below the knee and around the ball of the foot','Elastic strip: plantar foot over the middle calcaneus to the leg','Strip from the 5th metatarsal head over the medial calcaneus','Strip from the 1st metatarsal head over the lateral calcaneus','2–3 circular strips around the foot','4–6 strips around the lower leg'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Taping decisions',{a:{text:'You are taping an ankle for an inversion sprain. Which direction does each stirrup pull?',choices:[['From medial to lateral, pulling the foot toward eversion','b','Correct. Stirrups resist inversion.',true],['From lateral to medial, pulling into inversion','b','That would reproduce the injury mechanism.',false]]},b:{text:'After taping, the athlete’s toes look pale and feel cold. What do you do?',choices:[['Remove and reapply the tape with less tension, then recheck circulation','c','Correct. Tape must not cut off circulation.',true],['Leave it—tight tape means more support','c','Constriction is dangerous.',false]]},c:{text:'The new tape job has even, moderate tension and good circulation. The head athletic trainer approves it.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Rehab phases: protection, ROM, strength, neuromuscular control, sport-specific training, then return to play with criteria and clearance; use cryotherapy for acute injury and heat for chronic stiffness.','Taping: demonstrate adherent, heel and lace pads with lubricant, one smooth layer of pre-wrap, mild to moderate tension, follow the contour, overlap ½, check circulation and remove with scissors or a cutter.','Skill III-A: foot dorsiflexed, anchors below the gastrocnemius and at the midfoot, three stirrups (medial to lateral) and horseshoes overlapping ½, closures, heel locks, no pressure on the fifth metatarsal; Skill III-B: pain-free position, anchors, elastic strips over the middle, medial and lateral calcaneus, circular strips on foot and leg.'],terms:[['Stirrup','A tape strip running from the medial leg under the foot to the lateral leg.'],['Heel lock','A tape pattern that locks the heel to limit inversion and eversion.'],['Pre-wrap','A thin foam underwrap applied before tape to protect skin.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill III-A, how is the foot positioned?','In dorsiflexion','In plantarflexion','In inversion','Hanging relaxed','About 90°.'),q('Where are the lower-leg anchors placed for ankle taping?','Just below the belly of the gastrocnemius','Just above the knee','On the malleoli','Around the toes','Slight angle.'),q('How much should tape strips overlap?','About one-half the tape width','Not at all','Two full widths','One-tenth','For the basket weave.'),q('Which bone must tape not press on?','Fifth metatarsal head','Calcaneus','Tibia','Talus','Common irritation site.'),q('In Achilles technique two, the foot is held in:','The pain-free range','Maximum dorsiflexion','Full inversion','Eversion','Found by slowly dorsiflexing.'),q('What type of tape is used for the Achilles strips?','Heavyweight elastic tape','Non-elastic white tape only','Paper tape','Duct tape','Allows motion with support.'),q('Tape adherent in the HOSA skills is:','Demonstrated without actually spraying the patient','Sprayed heavily','Skipped entirely','Applied to the tape only','Per the rating sheet.'),q('Which modality is best for an acute sprain?','Cryotherapy','Moist heat','Hot whirlpool','Paraffin','Reduces swelling.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to tape an ankle for an inversion sprain following HOSA Skill III-A, from positioning through heel locks and removal, and explain why the tape is applied this way.',rubric:[['Preparation',['dorsiflexion','adherent','demonstrate','heel','lace','lubricant','pre-wrap']],['Anchors and basket weave',['anchors','gastrocnemius','midfoot','fifth metatarsal','stirrup','medial','lateral','horseshoe','½']],['Closure and heel locks',['closure','midfoot','heel lock','lateral calcaneus','both sides','2-3']],['Principles and removal',['eversion','resist inversion','tension','contour','circulation','scissors','report']]],model:'After obtaining instructions, assembling supplies, performing hand hygiene and identifying the patient, I seat the athlete on the taping table with the leg extended off the edge and the foot in dorsiflexion, which holds the ankle in its most stable position. I demonstrate spraying tape adherent without spraying, place thin foam pads with skin lubricant over the heel and lace areas to prevent blisters and apply one thin, smooth layer of pre-wrap. Using non-elastic tape, I apply two anchors at a slight angle around the distal lower leg just below the gastrocnemius belly and one around the midfoot proximal to the fifth metatarsal head. I apply the first stirrup from the medial anchor down behind the medial malleolus, under the foot and up behind the lateral malleolus to the lateral anchor, pulling slightly toward eversion to resist inversion, then the first horseshoe from the medial midfoot around the distal Achilles to the lateral midfoot. I alternate two more stirrups and horseshoes, each overlapping by half the tape width, to form a closed basket weave, then add closure strips up the leg and two or three around the midfoot, keeping pressure off the fifth metatarsal head. I apply heel locks on both sides—across the lace area, under the arch, up across the calcaneus and around the heel—to lock the subtalar joint. All tape uses mild to moderate tension and follows the skin’s contour; I check circulation, report to the head athletic trainer and, when directed, remove the tape with bandage scissors and dispose of it.'},sources:[RULES,OSP_REHAB,NIAMS_SPRAIN]};

export const SM10={title:'Shoulder spica and wrist/hand taping, bracing and padding (Skills III-C and III-D)',hook:'A hockey player with a strained shoulder needs support that does not restrict breathing. A gymnast with a sprained wrist needs to limit painful motion without cutting off circulation to the hand. Elastic wraps, figure-eight patterns and the right tension solve both problems. This lesson completes the Round Two taping skills and covers bracing and padding.',objectives:['Apply a shoulder spica wrap following HOSA Skill III-C.','Tape the wrist and hand with circular and figure-eight patterns following HOSA Skill III-D.','Choose braces and pads for common sports injuries.','Avoid common taping and wrapping errors.'],sections:[
['Elastic wraps and spica patterns',[
'**Elastic wraps** provide **compression** (to limit swelling) and **support**, and hold pads or ice packs in place. A **spica** is a figure-eight pattern that wraps around a limb and the trunk (shoulder spica) or the thigh and hip (hip spica). Wraps are applied from **distal to proximal** when used for compression, overlapping by about half the width, with **moderate, even tension**—too tight can cause numbness, color changes and swelling beyond the wrap. In Skill III-C, the wrap may be done over a T-shirt or tank top.',
'A **shoulder spica** supports **glenohumeral sprains and strains**, holds a **pad** over an AC sprain or contusion and secures an **ice pack**. The pattern must not constrict the **axilla** (armpit) of either side.',
chk('Why must a shoulder spica avoid constricting the axilla?',['Nerves and blood vessels pass through the armpit','It looks better','It makes the wrap last longer','The rating sheet does not mention it'],'Constriction can cause numbness and circulation problems.')]],
['Shoulder spica: Skill III-C',[
'Skill III-C has 20 steps in **5 minutes**. **1–5.** Obtain instructions, assemble equipment, hand hygiene, greet, introduce yourself, identify the patient and explain. **6.** Ask the patient to **stand** with the **hand of the involved arm resting on the lateral hip** in a relaxed manner. **7.** Place the **pad or ice pack** (as the scenario indicates) over the injured area. **8.** Anchor the extended end of the wrap on the **mid-to-proximal lateral upper arm** and wrap around the upper arm in a **medial** direction to encircle the anchor.',
'**9.** At the posterior upper arm, continue medially **over the lateral shoulder**, **across the chest**, **under the axilla of the non-involved arm** and **across the upper back**. **10.** Continue over the lateral involved shoulder, under the axilla and around the upper arm. **11.** Repeat the spica pattern **2–4 times**, overlapping slightly. **12.** Finish over the involved shoulder, upper back or thorax. **13.** Anchor **elastic tape** at the end of the wrap and apply **1–2 spica patterns of tape** over the wrap. **14.** Report to the head athletic trainer. **15.** On direction, remove with bandage scissors or a tape cutter. **16.** Wrap tension is **moderate** without constricting or irritating the axillae. **17–20.** Correct side and materials, disposal, hand hygiene and communication.',
order('Shoulder spica sequence',['Patient stands; involved hand on the lateral hip','Place the pad or ice pack','Anchor around the upper arm, wrapping medially','Over the shoulder, across the chest, under the opposite axilla','Across the upper back, back over the involved shoulder','Repeat 2–4 times, overlapping slightly','Finish and secure with 1–2 spicas of elastic tape','Report; remove on direction'])]],
['Wrist and hand taping: Skill III-D',[
'Skill III-D has 21 steps in **4 minutes**. **1–5.** Introductory steps. **6.** The patient sits with the **wrist in neutral** and the **fingers abducted** (spread)—spreading the fingers keeps the tape from becoming too tight when the hand relaxes. **7.** Demonstrate spraying adherent. **8.** Apply pre-wrap to the wrist and hand. **9.** Anchor **non-elastic tape over the ulnar styloid process** and continue in a **circular, lateral-to-medial** direction around the wrist, returning to the anchor. **10.** Apply **4–5 additional circular strips** around the wrist, overlapping by **½ the tape width** (individually or continuously).',
'**11.** Begin again at the **ulnar styloid**, run the tape **medially over the dorsum of the hand**, through the **thenar web space** (creasing the tape there to prevent constriction) and across the **distal palm**. **12.** Continue from the **fifth metacarpal** over the dorsum to the **distal radius**, around the wrist and back to the ulnar styloid. **13.** Repeat this **figure-eight**, overlapping by **⅓ the tape width**, and anchor on the dorsal wrist. **14–15.** Report and remove on direction. **16.** Tension is moderate without constricting the hand and thumb. **17.** Tape stays **proximal to the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints** so the knuckles can bend. **18–21.** Correct side and materials, disposal, hand hygiene and communication.',
order('Wrist and hand taping',['Wrist neutral, fingers spread','Demonstrate adherent; apply pre-wrap','Anchor over the ulnar styloid; circle the wrist lateral to medial','4–5 more circular strips, overlapping ½','Figure eight: dorsum of hand, thenar web space, distal palm','Fifth metacarpal back over the dorsum, around the wrist','Repeat the figure eight, overlapping ⅓; anchor dorsally','Keep tape proximal to the MCP joints']),
flip('Wrist/hand checkpoints',[['Wrist position','Neutral'],['Fingers','Abducted (spread) during taping'],['Anchor','Over the ulnar styloid process'],['Circular strips','4–5, overlapping ½'],['Figure-eight overlap','About ⅓ the tape width'],['Distal limit','Proximal to the MCP joints']])]],
['Bracing and padding',[
'**Braces** support or protect joints: **lace-up and stirrup ankle braces** prevent recurrent sprains; **functional knee braces** support ACL-deficient or reconstructed knees; **prophylactic knee braces** are used by some football linemen; **hinged elbow braces** limit extension after injury; **wrist splints** protect sprains and fractures; **shoulder harnesses** limit abduction and external rotation after dislocation. Braces must fit properly and are checked regularly.',
'**Padding** absorbs and spreads impact: **donut pads** relieve pressure around a bruise or blister; **felt and foam** cushion contusions; **thermoplastic** pads can be molded for hard protection (rules in some sports require them to be covered with soft padding). Custom **orthotics** support foot arches; **mouth guards** and **face shields** protect the face and teeth. Always follow sport rules on what padding and braces are allowed.',
match('Brace or pad and use',[['Lace-up ankle brace','Prevents recurrent ankle sprains'],['Functional knee brace','Supports an ACL-deficient knee'],['Hinged elbow brace','Limits extension'],['Donut pad','Relieves pressure around a bruise or blister'],['Thermoplastic pad','Molded hard protection'],['Shoulder harness','Limits abduction and external rotation']])]],
['Avoiding common errors',[
'Common taping errors include **wrinkles and gaps** (cause blisters and weak support), **too much tension** (cuts off circulation), **too little tension** (no support), **taping the wrong side**, **missing pads** over friction areas, tape that crosses joints it should not (such as the MCP joints) and forgetting the final report and removal steps. Always confirm the injured side in the scenario, check circulation and sensation afterward and ask how the tape feels.',
kase('Wrist taping check',{a:{text:'After taping, the athlete’s fingers tingle and the thumb web feels pinched. What happened? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['The tape is too tight or was not creased through the thenar web space','b','Correct. Crease the tape and use moderate tension.',true],['That is normal support','b','Tingling means the tape is too tight.',false]]},b:{text:'You also notice the tape covers the knuckles. What should you change?',choices:[['Keep the figure eight proximal to the MCP joints','c','Correct. The knuckles must bend freely.',true],['Add more tape over the knuckles','c','Tape should stay proximal to the MCP joints.',false]]},c:{text:'You remove and reapply the tape with the fingers spread, crease it through the web space and keep it off the knuckles.',end:true}})]],
["Choosing the right technique",["Match the support to the problem and the sport. **Non-elastic tape** limits motion at small, bony joints such as the ankle and wrist. **Elastic tape and wraps** support muscles and areas that change shape during activity, such as the Achilles, thigh and shoulder. **Compression wraps** control swelling after acute injuries. **Braces** are reusable and adjustable and are often preferred for long-term prevention; **tape** offers custom support for a single practice or game but loosens with sweat and activity.","Always consider the athlete’s **skin** (allergies, irritation, blisters), the **rules** of the sport, comfort and how the support affects performance. Tape and braces do not replace rehabilitation; they support athletes while strength, balance and control return.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Best choice","pairs":[["Ankle with recurrent sprains during a season","Lace-up brace or closed basket weave tape"],["Achilles strain","Elastic tape (technique two)"],["Shoulder strain needing an ice pack held in place","Shoulder spica wrap"],["Wrist sprain in a gymnast","Circular and figure-eight wrist tape"],["Swelling after an acute sprain","Compression wrap"],["Contusion that needs protection","Donut or foam pad"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Elastic wraps give compression and support; spicas are figure-eight patterns around a limb and trunk; use moderate tension and avoid constricting the axilla.','Skill III-C: patient stands with the involved hand on the hip, pad or ice pack, anchor on the upper arm wrapping medially, over the shoulder, across the chest, under the opposite axilla and across the back, repeat 2–4 times, secure with 1–2 tape spicas.','Skill III-D: wrist neutral and fingers spread, anchor over the ulnar styloid, 4–5 circular strips overlapping ½, figure eights through the thenar web space overlapping ⅓, tape proximal to the MCP joints; choose braces and pads that fit and follow sport rules.'],terms:[['Spica','A figure-eight wrap around a limb and the trunk.'],['Ulnar styloid process','The bony bump on the pinky side of the wrist.'],['Thenar web space','The skin between the thumb and index finger.']],mcqs:[q('How should the patient stand for a shoulder spica?','With the involved hand on the lateral hip','With both arms overhead','With the arm across the chest','Lying down','Relaxed position.'),q('In Skill III-C, the wrap passes under the axilla of the:','Non-involved arm','Involved arm only','Neither arm','Both arms at once','Then across the upper back.'),q('How many spica patterns are repeated with the wrap?','2–4','10–12','1','None','Overlapping slightly.'),q('How are the fingers positioned for wrist taping?','Abducted (spread)','Tightly closed in a fist','Flexed','Crossed','Prevents constriction.'),q('Where is the wrist tape anchored first?','Over the ulnar styloid process','On the thumb','Over the MCP joints','On the forearm muscle belly','Then circle lateral to medial.'),q('The figure-eight strips overlap by about:','One-third of the tape width','Two widths','None','One-half always','Per Skill III-D.'),q('The wrist/hand tape should stay:','Proximal to the MCP joints','Over the fingertips','Over the knuckles','On the elbow','Knuckles must bend.'),q('Tingling fingers after taping suggest:','The tape is too tight','Good support','The tape is too loose','Normal healing','Remove and reapply.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to tape the wrist and hand with circular and figure-eight patterns following HOSA Skill III-D, and describe how you would check that the tape is safe and effective.',rubric:[['Positioning and prep',['neutral','fingers','abducted','spread','adherent','pre-wrap']],['Circular strips',['ulnar styloid','lateral-to-medial','4-5','½','circular']],['Figure eight',['dorsum','thenar web','crease','distal palm','fifth metacarpal','distal radius','⅓','dorsal wrist']],['Safety checks',['moderate tension','constriction','mcp','circulation','sensation','report','scissors']]],model:'After obtaining instructions, assembling equipment, performing hand hygiene, greeting and identifying the patient and explaining the skill, I have the patient sit with the wrist in a neutral position and the fingers spread apart, so the tape will not tighten when the hand relaxes. I demonstrate spraying tape adherent without actually spraying and apply pre-wrap to the wrist and hand. Using non-elastic tape, I anchor over the ulnar styloid process and circle the wrist in a lateral-to-medial direction back to the anchor, then add four or five more circular strips, each overlapping by half the tape width. For the figure eight, I start again at the ulnar styloid, bring the tape medially over the back of the hand, through the thenar web space—creasing it there so it does not constrict the thumb—and across the distal palm, then from the fifth metacarpal back over the dorsum to the distal radius, around the wrist and back to the ulnar styloid. I repeat the figure eight, overlapping by about one-third of the tape width, and anchor on the dorsal wrist. To check safety and effectiveness, I confirm that the tension is moderate, the hand and thumb are not constricted and the tape stays proximal to the metacarpophalangeal joints so the knuckles can bend. I check the fingers’ color, warmth, capillary refill and sensation, ask the athlete how it feels and whether painful motion is limited, report to the head athletic trainer and remove the tape with bandage scissors when directed.'},sources:[RULES,NATA,AAOS_SCAPHOID]};
